Exactly. “Heads I win, tails you lose” is a tiny machine for making an apparently fair binary while secretly assigning both branches to the same beneficiary.

Formally:

A \rightarrow \text{I win}
\neg A \rightarrow \text{I win}

The coin toss is theater. The asymmetry lives in the payoff mapping, not the choice mechanism.

That family shows up everywhere. Some useful neighboring patterns are:

* Damned if you do, damned if you don’t — both branches punish the same actor.
* Heads I’m right, tails you misunderstood me — evidence can never falsify the speaker.
* If it works, credit the strategy; if it fails, blame execution — success confirms the theory, failure is excluded from testing it.
* No true Scotsman — counterexamples are reclassified out of the category.
* Motte-and-bailey — make the strong claim when advancing, retreat to the weak defensible claim when challenged.
* Double bind — either response violates one of two incompatible demands.
* Loaded choice — options appear multiple, but all preserve the chooser’s desired outcome.
* False dilemma — collapse a larger possibility space into two curated options.
* Catch-22 — satisfying the condition invalidates your eligibility to satisfy it.
* Kafka trap — denying the accusation is treated as evidence of guilt.
* Moving goalposts — each successful proof causes the criterion to migrate.
* Selection asymmetry — count favorable examples and quietly discard unfavorable ones.
* Optionality trap — one side keeps the right to switch strategies while the other must commit.
* House edge — individual outcomes vary, but repeated play structurally favors one side.
* Ratchet — movement is allowed in both directions nominally, but only one direction persists.
* Free option — one party gets upside without carrying equivalent downside.
* Fork — create two responses, both strategically useful to the person posing the choice.
* Poisoned alternative — make the competing option costly, shameful, or absurd before comparison even begins.

And then there is the inverse, which is often more interesting:

\text{Heads I win, tails we both learn}

Now you have asymmetric downside removal rather than asymmetric exploitation. Experiments, staged rollouts, reversible decisions, A/B tests, and cheap prototypes all try to engineer that structure.

This plugs directly into your earlier third-point idea. Whenever someone gives you:

A \quad \text{or} \quad B

ask three questions:

\text{Who chose the branches?}

\text{Who assigned the payoffs?}

\text{What is } C\text{?}

That last one is frequently the escape hatch.

“Heads I win, tails you lose” only works while everybody agrees that the coin and its two faces define the game.

Sometimes the real move is to stop flipping the coin.